{
  "text": " aside from the direct money that comes in through the NIH. But to mandate that people take medicine that they may not want or need is a very heavy decision. You can be completely for vaccines, completely for Pfizer. Exactly. Benefiting from, you can be a big Pfizer shareholder and you don't necessarily support forcing Pfizer products on people, don't you think that's a big difference? Well, I totally agree. And I published a piece before the mandate came out when they were talking about it in the Wall Street Journal explaining why the university vaccine mandates and universities were sort of first out of the gate with these mandates, why they violated medical ethics. And so I was already public as having put a stake in the ground on this issue, not anti-vaccine, but pro-informed consent. If people wanted to get the vaccine, I would be first to support their right to access it. In fact, in the early days of the vaccine rollout, I went public advocating in the LA Times and elsewhere that the poor and underinsured and marginalized communities get access to the vaccine if they wanted the vaccine.",
